// result_end

> Results

```
=== Library sort ===
input  (first 12): 70 53 44 63 12 33 12 76 70 81 48 95
sorted (first 12): 0 1 1 8 9 12 12 13 14 16 17 18
fully sorted? yes   (40 elements)

wrote library_sort.svg
```

The shuffled input comes back fully ordered \-\- matching `std::sort` over thousands of random arrays in the
test suite \-\- with each element having slid into a nearby gap rather than shoving the whole array aside.


The gray input scatters randomly while the blue output rises in a clean monotone staircase. The payoff is
invisible in the result but real in the work done to get there: by leaving gaps and rebalancing
occasionally, library sort turns insertion sort's \(O(n^2)\) shifting into \(O(n\log n)\) expected time,
keeping the algorithm's one-at-a-time simplicity while shedding its cost.
